    "complete": true,
    "clarification_required": false,
    "rationale": "I read two records, and both passed the current-record checks. They are repository-scoped, active, reviewed, visible and not superseded. 'Pagination cursor: empty page' (6993a696, v1) answers the first question. An empty page that still has a cursor does not end the scan. Keep fetching with the returned cursor until the server stops sending one. 'Pagination cursor: repeated token' (c7b17274, v1) answers the second question. What prevents a loop is checking for a cursor that comes back again. If one does, stop the scan with a diagnostic and keep the last completed page. I reached both through the only route about pagination continuation and repeated-cursor protection: MEMORY.md -> topics/g/pagination.md -> topics/n/pagination-0-1.md. No other route in the root map is about cursor pagination. Request-reliability covers retries and deadlines, and request-identity covers repeated requests, not cursors. Caveats: both records come from synthetic fixtures. Their source is listed as executable-test, and their admission reason says they are not real admission evidence. They are untrusted memory and should be checked against the actual code before anyone relies on them. They don't contradict each other: one says when to keep going, the other gives the guard against looping. Nothing was missing from coverage of this question."
  },
